1
00:00:00,256 --> 00:00:01,543
Hi, I'm Derric.

2
00:00:01,664 --> 00:00:02,589
This is Gridline.

3
00:00:02,773 --> 00:00:04,189
Let me show you how I'd use it

4
00:00:04,181 --> 00:00:06,066
to check a wind forecast against what actually

5
00:00:06,229 --> 00:00:06,877
happened.

6
00:00:07,296 --> 00:00:09,949
The dashboard covers decisions made during January twenty

7
00:00:10,027 --> 00:00:10,657
twenty-six.

8
00:00:10,846 --> 00:00:12,688
A decision on one day is matched with

9
00:00:12,681 --> 00:00:14,523
generation on the following day.

10
00:00:14,814 --> 00:00:17,125
Right now, the selected cutoff is three thirty

11
00:00:17,203 --> 00:00:18,896
in the afternoon, London time.

12
00:00:19,123 --> 00:00:20,773
I'll pick the tenth of January.

13
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:22,808
The delivery date changes to the eleventh.

14
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:25,675
The green line is the forecast available at

15
00:00:25,838 --> 00:00:26,699
decision time.

16
00:00:26,863 --> 00:00:29,622
The dashed line is the actual generation.

17
00:00:29,934 --> 00:00:32,033
You can see that the forecast is consistently

18
00:00:32,153 --> 00:00:32,918
higher here.

19
00:00:33,113 --> 00:00:35,424
If I open the values underneath, I can

20
00:00:35,417 --> 00:00:37,984
check the actual numbers and when that forecast

21
00:00:38,019 --> 00:00:38,859
was published.

22
00:00:39,086 --> 00:00:41,077
That publication time matters.

23
00:00:41,305 --> 00:00:43,445
A later revision can't be used to judge

24
00:00:43,481 --> 00:00:44,663
an earlier decision.

25
00:00:44,935 --> 00:00:47,289
The monthly mean absolute error is about eighteen

26
00:00:47,281 --> 00:00:50,531
hundred and seventy-three megawatts, and the bias is

27
00:00:50,567 --> 00:00:51,321
positive.

28
00:00:51,676 --> 00:00:54,713
So in this snapshot, forecasts generally sit above

29
00:00:54,791 --> 00:00:55,663
the actuals.

30
00:00:55,923 --> 00:00:58,960
These are forecast-error measures, rather than a calculation

31
00:00:59,166 --> 00:01:00,560
of money saved.

32
00:01:00,958 --> 00:01:03,781
To find the days worth investigating, I go

33
00:01:03,773 --> 00:01:05,103
to Daily accuracy.

34
00:01:05,352 --> 00:01:07,749
The tenth of January stands out, with an

35
00:01:07,784 --> 00:01:10,608
average error of roughly forty-eight hundred and eighty-one

36
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:11,403
megawatts.

37
00:01:11,594 --> 00:01:13,734
I can open that row and go straight

38
00:01:13,770 --> 00:01:15,292
back to its chart.

39
00:01:15,647 --> 00:01:17,873
The date selection also handles the end of

40
00:01:17,866 --> 00:01:20,817
the month: the thirty-first of January maps to

41
00:01:20,810 --> 00:01:22,758
delivery on the first of February.

42
00:01:23,071 --> 00:01:25,169
One detail I wanted to make clear is

43
00:01:25,204 --> 00:01:26,376
the time resolution.

44
00:01:26,638 --> 00:01:28,694
On Method and sources, you can see that

45
00:01:28,686 --> 00:01:31,936
the forecasts are hourly, while the actual observations

46
00:01:32,142 --> 00:01:33,472
are half-hourly.

47
00:01:33,806 --> 00:01:36,715
The app averages both half-hours for an hourly

48
00:01:36,835 --> 00:01:37,632
comparison.

49
00:01:37,945 --> 00:01:40,862
It doesn't measure half-hour forecast accuracy.

50
00:01:41,119 --> 00:01:43,388
The two datasets also aren't proven to cover

51
00:01:43,509 --> 00:01:45,159
exactly the same wind assets.

52
00:01:45,386 --> 00:01:48,209
Underneath, it's a React frontend with a Fast

53
00:01:48,287 --> 00:01:51,253
A P I backend reading archived Elexon records.

54
00:01:51,474 --> 00:01:54,511
The important safeguard is selecting only forecast publications

55
00:01:54,759 --> 00:01:56,175
available by the cutoff.

56
00:01:56,466 --> 00:01:59,161
The existing Python and CSV checks passed during

57
00:01:59,239 --> 00:01:59,953
validation.

58
00:02:00,183 --> 00:02:02,580
That's Gridline: I can start with the monthly

59
00:02:02,615 --> 00:02:05,396
picture, find a difficult day, and inspect the

60
00:02:05,389 --> 00:02:07,948
forecast and source details behind it.

